Runbook 2: Database Migration Failure & Recovery
Context
A non-backward-compatible database migration job fails halfway through, locking tables or erroring out.
Steps
1. Identify the failing migration pod:
kubectl get pods -n shared -l job-name=db-migration
kubectl logs -n shared job/db-migration

2. Roll back application traffic to stable version:
kubectl argo rollouts abort shared-api -n shared
kubectl argo rollouts undo shared-api -n shared

3. Connect to PgBouncer / PostgreSQL directly:
kubectl exec -it postgres-postgresql-0 -n datastores -- psql -U postgres -d app

4. Query locks and drop blocking migrations:
SELECT pid, query, state FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state != 'idle';

5. Apply compensation script (Expand/Contract inverse script).


Runbook 3: Disaster Recovery Restore Test
Context
Validate automated point-in-time recovery (PITR) for PostgreSQL.

Steps
Create a snapshot backup:
1. kubectl exec postgres-postgresql-0 -n datastores -- pg_dump -U postgres app > /tmp/backup_test.sql

2. Inject a simulated failure:
kubectl exec postgres-postgresql-0 -n datastores -- psql -U postgres -d app -c "DROP TABLE users;"

3. Restore database from backup:
cat /tmp/backup_test.sql | kubectl exec -i postgres-postgresql-0 -n datastores -- psql -U postgres -d app

4. Verify table integrity:
kubectl exec postgres-postgresql-0 -n datastores -- psql -U postgres -d app -c "SELECT COUNT(*) FROM users;"

